<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federico da Montefeltro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1422">1422</a>–<a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Federico da Montefeltro was lord of Urbino from 1444 and its first duke from 1474, and one of the most successful condottieri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, he sacked Volterra for Florence in 1472. He built the Ducal Palace of Urbino and a famous library, making his court a centre of humanist culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro.jpg" alt="Federico da Montefeltro" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piero della Francesca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federico_da_Montefeltro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
